1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,819
A car-parts company selling five billion dollars a year

2
00:00:03,819 --> 00:00:07,421
went bankrupt with twelve million dollars in the bank.

3
00:00:08,083 --> 00:00:10,792
And more than nine billion dollars of debts.

4
00:00:11,383 --> 00:00:14,072
You've probably used its products.

5
00:00:14,072 --> 00:00:15,417
Fram oil filters.

6
00:00:15,417 --> 00:00:17,078
Autolite spark plugs.

7
00:00:17,078 --> 00:00:18,423
Raybestos brakes.

8
00:00:19,016 --> 00:00:22,157
Prosecutors say the money was raised with

9
00:00:22,157 --> 00:00:25,528
fake invoices and debts hidden from lenders.

10
00:00:25,528 --> 00:00:28,286
The founder and his brother deny it.

11
00:00:28,286 --> 00:00:31,121
Their trial is set for February 2027.

12
00:00:31,783 --> 00:00:33,480
So where did the money go?

13
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:37,201
And how did some of the biggest names in finance miss it?

14
00:00:37,201 --> 00:00:39,420
Keep that second question in mind.

15
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:44,538
We use the court record: the US Attorney's charges, bankruptcy

16
00:00:44,538 --> 00:00:49,068
court filings, lenders' own statements, and reporting on the case.

17
00:00:49,068 --> 00:00:52,225
Where something is only alleged, we'll say so.

18
00:00:52,883 --> 00:00:53,692
Chapter 1.

19
00:00:53,692 --> 00:00:55,635
Built on borrowed money.

20
00:00:56,283 --> 00:01:00,897
Patrick James founded the business in Ohio in 2013.

21
00:01:00,897 --> 00:01:03,792
It was then called Crowne Group.

22
00:01:04,383 --> 00:01:07,716
It grew by buying parts makers, wiper brand

23
00:01:07,716 --> 00:01:11,205
Trico among them, mostly with borrowed money.

24
00:01:11,205 --> 00:01:15,313
In August 2020 the group renamed itself First Brands.

25
00:01:15,983 --> 00:01:19,593
From the outside, it looked boring in a good way.

26
00:01:19,593 --> 00:01:23,129
Cars always need filters, wipers and brake pads.

27
00:01:23,716 --> 00:01:27,118
But a business like that has a cash problem built in.

28
00:01:27,118 --> 00:01:31,097
And the way it solved that problem is the heart of this story.

29
00:01:31,683 --> 00:01:32,437
Chapter 2.

30
00:01:32,437 --> 00:01:34,776
Selling tomorrow's money today.

31
00:01:35,416 --> 00:01:40,737
When a parts maker ships to a retailer, the retailer often pays months later.

32
00:01:40,737 --> 00:01:43,502
That unpaid bill is called a receivable.

33
00:01:44,116 --> 00:01:48,101
Instead of waiting, the company can sell the bill to a finance

34
00:01:48,101 --> 00:01:51,571
firm, called a factor, for a bit less than it's worth.

35
00:01:51,571 --> 00:01:54,271
The factor collects the full amount later.

36
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:57,228
This is legal and very common.

37
00:01:57,228 --> 00:02:03,227
A related version, used to pay suppliers early, is called supply-chain finance.

38
00:02:03,816 --> 00:02:06,554
It only works if two things are true.

39
00:02:06,554 --> 00:02:08,035
The invoice is real.

40
00:02:08,035 --> 00:02:09,811
And it's sold only once.

41
00:02:10,416 --> 00:02:12,328
Hold on to those two rules.

42
00:02:12,328 --> 00:02:15,728
Prosecutors say First Brands broke both of them.

43
00:02:16,383 --> 00:02:17,088
Chapter 3.

44
00:02:17,088 --> 00:02:20,052
The deal that asked one question too many.

45
00:02:20,716 --> 00:02:26,239
By August 2025, First Brands had about five point six billion dollars of

46
00:02:26,239 --> 00:02:31,839
corporate debt on its balance sheet, and a refinancing deal in the works.

47
00:02:32,516 --> 00:02:37,864
Investors asked for a deeper check, including a quality-of-earnings report.

48
00:02:37,864 --> 00:02:40,860
According to the investment firm OHA, that

49
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:45,138
review uncovered increasingly risky and expensive financing.

50
00:02:45,816 --> 00:02:47,075
The deal collapsed.

51
00:02:47,075 --> 00:02:50,123
Within three weeks, the price of the company's

52
00:02:50,123 --> 00:02:53,238
main loan fell by nearly eighty-three per cent.

53
00:02:53,916 --> 00:02:57,304
OHA said some of the off-balance-sheet financing

54
00:02:57,304 --> 00:03:00,480
carried interest rates above thirty per cent.

55
00:03:01,150 --> 00:03:05,013
On the twenty-eighth of September 2025, First

56
00:03:05,013 --> 00:03:08,189
Brands filed for bankruptcy in Texas.

57
00:03:08,883 --> 00:03:13,590
It reported about five billion dollars in annual sales, twelve million

58
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:18,096
dollars in cash, and more than nine billion dollars in liabilities.

59
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:19,478
Chapter 4.

60
00:03:19,478 --> 00:03:22,248
Up to two point three billion dollars.

61
00:03:22,883 --> 00:03:27,525
Ten days after the filing, Raistone, a trade-finance firm and one of

62
00:03:27,525 --> 00:03:32,713
the company's creditors, asked the court to appoint an independent examiner.

63
00:03:33,383 --> 00:03:35,890
Its filing said up to two point three

64
00:03:35,890 --> 00:03:39,075
billion dollars in assets were unaccounted for.

65
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:43,438
It said that after the bankruptcy began, the company had

66
00:03:43,438 --> 00:03:47,587
collected about one point nine billion dollars on invoices that

67
00:03:47,587 --> 00:03:50,946
others had already bought, but hadn't passed it on.

68
00:03:51,683 --> 00:03:54,452
Asked how much was in the accounts meant to

69
00:03:54,452 --> 00:03:58,124
hold that money, a company lawyer answered: zero dollars.

70
00:03:58,124 --> 00:04:01,666
Asked whether the company had actually received the one

71
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:04,951
point nine billion, its lawyer said: we don't know.

72
00:04:05,683 --> 00:04:07,246
Big names were exposed.

73
00:04:07,246 --> 00:04:13,431
Jefferies said its share of one affected fund's First Brands invoices was about forty-three

74
00:04:13,431 --> 00:04:18,052
million dollars, and that nobody at the bank had known of any fraud.

75
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:19,604
Chapter 5.

76
00:04:19,604 --> 00:04:20,935
The indictment.

77
00:04:21,583 --> 00:04:27,033
On the twenty-ninth of January 2026, federal prosecutors in New York

78
00:04:27,033 --> 00:04:32,804
charged Patrick James and his brother Edward, a former senior executive.

79
00:04:32,804 --> 00:04:35,689
Both were arrested in Ohio that day.

80
00:04:36,383 --> 00:04:37,982
These are accusations.

81
00:04:37,982 --> 00:04:41,908
Both men pleaded not guilty and are presumed innocent.

82
00:04:42,516 --> 00:04:48,632
Prosecutors allege that from at least 2018, the company used fake and inflated

83
00:04:48,632 --> 00:04:54,512
invoices to raise money from factors, including on bills that didn't exist.

84
00:04:55,183 --> 00:04:58,747
One example in court filings: an invoice worth eight

85
00:04:58,747 --> 00:05:03,134
thousand nine hundred and seventy-six dollars was allegedly sold

86
00:05:03,134 --> 00:05:06,630
to one factor for almost eighteen thousand dollars.

87
00:05:07,383 --> 00:05:11,644
Three days later, prosecutors say, the same invoice was sold to

88
00:05:11,644 --> 00:05:16,446
another factor for nearly four hundred and sixty-four thousand dollars.

89
00:05:17,116 --> 00:05:21,601
They also allege the same collateral was pledged two or three times,

90
00:05:21,601 --> 00:05:25,690
so several lenders thought they had a claim on the same thing.

91
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:28,528
Then there were the round trips.

92
00:05:28,528 --> 00:05:33,770
Money that financers thought would pay suppliers was allegedly routed back to

93
00:05:33,770 --> 00:05:38,740
First Brands, prosecutors say, to pay debt, rent and other running costs.

94
00:05:39,416 --> 00:05:43,704
Internally, the release says, these were called corporate initiatives.

95
00:05:44,316 --> 00:05:49,227
Prosecutors also allege that companies controlled by Patrick James took

96
00:05:49,227 --> 00:05:53,378
on inventory loans that were hidden from the senior lenders.

97
00:05:54,050 --> 00:05:58,324
In July 2025, prosecutors allege, a senior executive,

98
00:05:58,324 --> 00:06:02,518
at Patrick James's direction, told lenders there was

99
00:06:02,518 --> 00:06:07,841
no off-balance-sheet financing involving special-purpose entities.

100
00:06:07,841 --> 00:06:12,278
Prosecutors put such debt at about two billion dollars.

101
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:16,861
Prosecutors say Patrick James received at least hundreds

102
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:20,128
of millions of dollars in his personal accounts.

103
00:06:20,128 --> 00:06:24,416
His spokesperson called the company's spending claims baseless.

104
00:06:25,083 --> 00:06:25,873
Chapter 6.

105
00:06:25,873 --> 00:06:27,771
Brother against brother.

106
00:06:28,416 --> 00:06:32,873
Two insiders have already pleaded guilty and agreed to

107
00:06:32,873 --> 00:06:38,155
cooperate: executive Peter Andrew Brumbergs in January 2026, and

108
00:06:38,155 --> 00:06:42,694
former chief financial officer Stephen Graham in March.

109
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:47,425
Graham had been finance chief since 2014.

110
00:06:47,425 --> 00:06:49,850
He's expected to testify.

111
00:06:50,450 --> 00:06:54,831
In September 2026, both brothers asked for separate trials.

112
00:06:54,831 --> 00:06:58,618
Patrick's lawyers say Edward ran the alleged fraud.

113
00:06:58,618 --> 00:07:04,188
Edward's lawyers say Patrick controlled the company and Edward didn't know.

114
00:07:04,783 --> 00:07:07,878
A jury will decide, not this video.

115
00:07:07,878 --> 00:07:11,503
The trial is scheduled for February 2027.

116
00:07:12,116 --> 00:07:13,194
Chapter 7.

117
00:07:13,194 --> 00:07:14,164
Who paid.

118
00:07:14,816 --> 00:07:18,264
In January 2026, the company began winding

119
00:07:18,264 --> 00:07:21,793
down Autolite, Brake Parts Inc and Cardone.

120
00:07:21,793 --> 00:07:27,129
Reuters reported those units employed about four thousand people.

121
00:07:27,816 --> 00:07:30,229
Other businesses were sold off.

122
00:07:30,229 --> 00:07:35,366
In late August 2026, the bankruptcy judge rejected the rescue plan

123
00:07:35,366 --> 00:07:40,036
and ordered the case converted into a Chapter 7 liquidation.

124
00:07:40,036 --> 00:07:43,461
In plain English: it's being sold in pieces.

125
00:07:44,149 --> 00:07:49,999
That rejected plan relied on about two billion dollars of future lawsuit

126
00:07:49,999 --> 00:07:55,930
recoveries, including from former insiders, projected by the end of 2028.

127
00:07:56,616 --> 00:07:57,896
Our analysis.

128
00:07:58,549 --> 00:07:59,332
Lesson one.

129
00:07:59,332 --> 00:08:01,038
Compare cash with sales.

130
00:08:01,038 --> 00:08:04,665
Five billion dollars of sales and twelve million in

131
00:08:04,665 --> 00:08:08,292
the bank means a company living invoice to invoice.

132
00:08:08,949 --> 00:08:09,643
Lesson two.

133
00:08:09,643 --> 00:08:11,724
Debt you can't see is still debt.

134
00:08:11,724 --> 00:08:15,949
Off-balance-sheet financing doesn't appear in the headline numbers.

135
00:08:15,949 --> 00:08:18,912
That's exactly why lenders should ask about it.

136
00:08:19,516 --> 00:08:20,400
Lesson three.

137
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:25,363
When every lender sees only its own slice, nobody sees the whole picture.

138
00:08:25,363 --> 00:08:28,626
In our view, no single factor had an easy way to

139
00:08:28,626 --> 00:08:32,910
check whether an invoice had already been sold to someone else.

140
00:08:33,583 --> 00:08:36,364
Hidden debt is a pattern on this channel.

141
00:08:36,364 --> 00:08:41,588
Patisserie Valerie's chairman said he hadn't known about two bank overdrafts.

142
00:08:41,588 --> 00:08:43,759
That case file is on screen now.

143
00:08:44,349 --> 00:08:47,361
The First Brands trial is set for February.

144
00:08:47,361 --> 00:08:51,283
Subscribe if you want the follow-up when a jury decides.
